{#
  One governed chart, written as template source.

  The elements are here rather than built as a string in Python for one reason:
  autoescaping is unconditional and nothing reachable from the bundle is ever
  marked safe, so a Python string of markup would reach the reader as `&lt;svg`.
  A macro's output is markup because the macro *is* source, while every label
  inside it still passes through the same escaping as a table cell -- which is
  what makes a product named `<script>` inert here for the same reason it is
  inert there.

  Coordinates come from `charts.build_chart` as exact decimal strings, and the
  viewBox is written from the view rather than from a literal, so a change to the
  canvas cannot leave this template drawing to the old one.

  `title_code` and `description_code` are governed codes, and so is a label whose
  `localize` flag is set. Every one of them is looked up in the per-language chrome,
  never printed raw: the environment uses `StrictUndefined`, so a code the chrome has
  no entry for raises here rather than reaching a reader as an identifier.

  The title reuses the section heading a reader already sees, so there is no second
  table to keep in step with the first.
#}
{% macro chart(view, section_id, chrome) %}
<svg role="img" aria-labelledby="{{ section_id }}-ct {{ section_id }}-cd"
     viewBox="0 0 {{ view.width }} {{ view.height }}"
     class="chart chart--{{ view.kind }}">
  <title id="{{ section_id }}-ct">{{ chrome.sections[section_id] }}</title>
  <desc id="{{ section_id }}-cd">{{ chrome.chart_descriptions[view.description_code] }}</desc>
  {#
    The zero line, drawn before the marks so a bar sits on top of it rather than
    being cut by it. Its position comes from the view: the domain always includes
    zero, so the line is always inside the canvas, and deriving the offset here
    again would be a second chance to disagree with the geometry.
  #}
  <line class="chart__baseline" x1="0" y1="{{ view.baseline }}"
        x2="{{ view.width }}" y2="{{ view.baseline }}" />
  {% for mark in view.marks %}
  <rect class="chart__mark" x="{{ mark.x }}" y="{{ mark.y }}"
        width="{{ mark.width }}" height="{{ mark.height }}" />
  {% endfor %}
  {% if view.polyline %}
  <polyline class="chart__curve" fill="none" points="{{ view.polyline }}" />
  {% endif %}
  {% for label in view.labels %}
  <text class="chart__label" x="{{ label.x }}" y="{{ label.y }}">{#
    #}{% if label.localize %}{{ chrome.labels[label.value] }}{#
    #}{% else %}{{ label.value }}{% endif %}</text>
  {% endfor %}
  {#
    The value axis states its unit. The word is a governed code resolved in the
    per-language chrome like every other one here, so a unit kind with no entry
    raises under `StrictUndefined` rather than reaching a reader as `monetary`.
    No period: nothing the bundle hands this chart carries one, and a chart that
    composed one would be stating a fact it was never given.
  #}
  <text class="chart__axis-unit" x="0" y="{{ view.height }}">{#
    #}{{ chrome.axis_units[view.axis_unit_kind] }}</text>
</svg>
{% endmacro %}
